WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.558
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.858 --> 00:00:10.314
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.614 --> 00:00:17.260
Unit twenty-two: UN Peacekeeping. Today you will report actions formally with the passive and no agent.

4
00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.224
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:24.423
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:26.354
Peacekeeping.

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.451
Peacekeeping means the use of international forces to help keep a peace agreement.

8
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.398
Peacekeeping reduced fighting in the region.

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:37.787
Your turn. Say: Peacekeeping.

10
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:41.704
Peacekeeping.

11
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.823
Blue helmets.

12
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:48.941
Blue helmets means the nickname for United Nations peacekeeping soldiers.

13
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.666
The blue helmets patrolled the border.

14
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.657
Your turn. Say: Blue helmets.

15
00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.803
Blue helmets.

16
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.760
Ceasefire.

17
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.131
Ceasefire means an agreement to stop fighting for a period.

18
00:01:06.717 --> 00:01:08.763
The ceasefire lasted only a week.

19
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.235
Your turn. Say: Ceasefire.

20
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.136
Ceasefire.

21
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:17.710
Mandate.

22
00:01:18.717 --> 00:01:23.085
Mandate means the official instructions that define a mission's tasks.

23
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:26.391
The mandate allowed troops to protect civilians.

24
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:29.473
Your turn. Say: Mandate.

25
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:33.242
Mandate.

26
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:34.857
Troops.

27
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.720
Troops means soldiers who serve together as a group.

28
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:41.699
Troops from many countries joined the mission.

29
00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:45.330
Your turn. Say: Troops.

30
00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:49.063
Troops.

31
00:01:49.817 --> 00:01:50.992
Civilians.

32
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:55.910
Civilians means people who are not members of the armed forces.

33
00:01:56.517 --> 00:01:59.120
Civilians were moved to safe areas.

34
00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:02.346
Your turn. Say: Civilians.

35
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.445
Civilians.

36
00:02:07.183 --> 00:02:08.138
Conflict.

37
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.218
Conflict means a serious fight or war between groups.

38
00:02:12.817 --> 00:02:15.095
The conflict lasted ten years.

39
00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:18.115
Your turn. Say: Conflict.

40
00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:21.898
Conflict.

41
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.255
Security Council.

42
00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:30.582
Security Council means the United Nations body responsible for international peace.

43
00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:33.531
The Security Council voted on the mission.

44
00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:36.978
Your turn. Say: Security Council.

45
00:02:39.833 --> 00:02:41.357
Security Council.

46
00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:45.841
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:52.296
Visal, a UN report says 'Troops were deployed and civilians were evacuated.' Who did these actions?

48
00:02:53.383 --> 00:03:04.908
The report does not say. The formal passive hides the agent because it is obvious or unimportant. The focus stays on the action and on the people affected.

49
00:03:06.017 --> 00:03:07.714
When should we mention the agent?

50
00:03:08.817 --> 00:03:21.236
When it matters: 'The ceasefire was broken by armed groups.' Use by + agent to show responsibility. Without it, the passive can sound neutral and sometimes hides blame.

51
00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.955
Which tenses are common?

52
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:36.584
Past passive for events (was deployed), present perfect for results (has been extended) and modal passive for rules (must be protected). Check the auxiliary agrees with the subject.

53
00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:39.222
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:43.348
Grammar focus: Formal passive (agent omitted).

55
00:03:43.648 --> 00:03:44.812
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.517 --> 00:03:47.981
Troops were deployed along the ceasefire line.

57
00:03:48.883 --> 00:03:51.742
The mandate has been extended by the Security Council.

58
00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.811
Civilians must be protected from violence at all times.

59
00:03:56.767 --> 00:04:05.781
Use be + past participle to focus on the action. Add by + agent only when it matters; omitting it can sound neutral but may hide responsibility.

60
00:04:06.517 --> 00:04:10.237
Be careful! Don't say: Troops was deployed to the border.

61
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.337
Say: Troops were deployed to the border.

62
00:04:15.637 --> 00:04:18.020
The auxiliary must agree with the subject.

63
00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:21.462
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:23.558
Keeping the Peace.

65
00:04:23.858 --> 00:04:28.493
After a conflict ends, a ceasefire must be monitored so that fighting does not restart.

66
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:35.865
Blue helmets are sent by the Security Council under a clear mandate, and troops are deployed to protect civilians.

67
00:04:36.867 --> 00:04:42.245
Mandates are sometimes written too broadly, so missions are asked to do more than their resources allow.

68
00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:49.343
Peacekeeping succeeds when local communities are consulted and when agreements are respected by all parties.

69
00:04:50.333 --> 00:04:55.003
It fails when civilians are left unprotected or when the mission is not supported.

70
00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.229
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.083 --> 00:05:04.035
The ceasefire blank by both sides yesterday.

72
00:05:04.335 --> 00:05:07.594
A, was signed. B, signed. C, were signed.

73
00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:12.936
The answer is A: was signed.

74
00:05:14.083 --> 00:05:15.781
Which sentence is passive?

75
00:05:16.081 --> 00:05:22.254
A, Troops protected the town.. B, The town was protected by troops.. C, Troops are protect the town..

76
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.216
The answer is B: The town was protected by troops..

77
00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:34.045
The official instructions that define a mission's tasks are its blank.

78
00:05:34.345 --> 00:05:37.348
A, mandate. B, tariff. C, aid.

79
00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:42.969
The answer is A: mandate.

80
00:05:44.117 --> 00:05:46.070
People who are not soldiers are blank.

81
00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:50.279
A, civilians. B, troops. C, delegates.

82
00:05:53.833 --> 00:05:56.204
The answer is A: civilians.

83
00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.592
According to the reading, which body sends the blue helmets?

84
00:06:00.892 --> 00:06:05.613
A, The Security Council. B, A trade bloc. C, A local bank.

85
00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:12.002
The answer is A: The Security Council.

86
00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.259
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:17.117 --> 00:06:20.091
What makes peacekeeping missions succeed or fail?

88
00:06:28.883 --> 00:06:30.291
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:30.591 --> 00:06:57.862
Peacekeeping succeeds when the mission has a clear mandate, enough troops and equipment, and the consent of the conflicting parties. Local trust is important, so civilians should be consulted and protected. Missions fail when mandates are unrealistic, funding is cut or the peace agreement is not respected. Political support from the Security Council matters as much as military strength.

90
00:06:58.162 --> 00:07:00.057
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:05.553
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and formal passive (agent omitted).

92
00:07:05.853 --> 00:07:09.306
Today's value is courage. Try new things and be brave.

93
00:07:09.606 --> 00:07:13.350
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:16.372
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

